            0.313,
            0.925,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "Magic square with numbers 3 – 11 can be created by adding 2 to each number of the magic square 1 – 9."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.355,
            0.925,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": "Magic square with numbers 9 – 17 can be created by adding 8 to each number of the magic square 1 – 9."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.407,
            0.926,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": "? **Choose any magic square that you have made so far using consecutive numbers. If m is the letter-number of the number in the centre, express how other numbers are related to m, how much more or less than m. [Hint: Remember, how we described a 2 × 2 grid of a calendar month in the Algebraic Expressions chapter].**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.103,
            0.511,
            0.925,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": "**Ans:** Taking ‘m’ as the letter-number of the number in the centre and expressing other numbers in relation to ‘m’."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.404,
            0.57,
            0.638,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "| m+ 3 | m-4 | m+ 1 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| m-2 | m | m+ 2 |\n| m-1 | m+ 4 | m-3 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_26_table_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
